  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 235 PM CDT Thu Aug 13 2026\n\nCurrent analysis depicts a 500-hPa ridge covering much of the\ncentral and eastern CONUS with troughing over both the far \neastern and western CONUS. At the surface, high pressure over\nthe Gulf and low pressure over Kansas continue to funnel Gulf\nmoisture into eastern KS and western MO resulting in the\npersistence of hot and humid conditions. High temperatures today\nand tomorrow are once again expected to be in the upper 90s, \nwith dew points in the mid-70s, leading to heat indices pushing\ntowards 110F. As such, the Extreme Heat Warning and Heat \nAdvisory remain in effect for eastern KS and northern/central MO\nthrough 8 PM on Friday. Cloud cover and an outflow boundary \nfrom convection in Iowa this morning has kept areas of far \nnorthern MO slightly cooler and below Heat Advisory criteria.\n\nA warm/stationary front extends northeast from the low pressure\ncenter in central KS into central IA. This boundary is \nexpected to drift south into northern MO this evening and be \nthe focus for storm development across central KS into the \novernight hours as a weak shortwave moves through the flow and \ninteracts with the LLJ. Current thinking is that a cap across \nnorthern MO will limit convective potential and the convection \nwill primarily remain to the west in central KS. However, recent\nruns of the CAMs have shifted the axis of storm development \nslightly further east. With PWATs sitting near 2\", storms will \nbe very efficient precipitation producers, so training storms \nalong the frontal boundary would pose a flash flooding threat, \nwith 3- and 6-hr flash flood guidance being in the 2-2.5\" and \n3-4\" range respectively. Aside from this, the severe threat with\nany storms is fairly low, though some small hail and a wind \ngust or two cannot be ruled out.\n\nA secondary axis of convective development is forecast further\nnorth early tomorrow morning, in central IA, closer to the \nheart of the forcing from the shortwave. This convection should \nlargely stay north of the IA/MO border, but it is possible that \na cell or two could straddle the state line bringing heavy \nrainfall chances to northern MO early tomorrow morning.\n\nOn Friday, the low pressure system is progged to lift into IA \nand towards the Great Lakes region, with northern MO and eastern\nKS remaining in the warm sector of the system. This will allow \nthe heat and humidity to continue into the weekend. The \nstationary front is expected to remain positioned across far \nnorthern MO through Saturday, with additional storm development \nbeing possible along the front, which may lead to a continued flash\nflooding threat into the weekend. However, confidence in timing\nand location of any storms remains low at this time. Storms and\nassociated cloud cover may limit the heat potential on Saturday\nacross northern MO, but the full impacts remain unclear. As \nsuch, the Extreme Heat Warning remains in effect across central \nMO through 9 PM on Saturday and has not been expanded northward \nas of now. A northward expansion may be needed in coming \nforecast cycles depending on the evolution of the previously \ndiscussed items.\n\nThe cold front of the low pressure system is expected to finally \npush southward on Sunday, bringing some much needed relief from\nthe heat and potentially a few showers and storms. Unfortunately,\nthe relief will not last very long, as ridging is expected to \nreturn by mid-week next week and a very similar pattern to the \ncurrent pattern emerges once again.",
